{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b 1. The Two Sides}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b 1.1 Fermat \u8212- Special Relativity, The Skeleton}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Fermat's Last Theorem: x^n + y^n = z^n has no integer solutions for n > 2.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the Wiles proof, this is established via the Taniyama-Shimura-Weil modularity theorem: every elliptic curve over Q is modular. A solution to Fermat would produce a non-modular elliptic curve (Frey, 1985). Contradiction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Fermat is Special Relativity because it is the boundary condition \u8212- the speed limit of the integer line. Just as nothing with mass can reach c, no integer triple can satisfy Fermat for n > 2. The constraint is absolute. The lattice is discrete. The measure of Fermat solutions inside the Riemann continuum is zero \u8212- Aleph-null against the uncountable.\par}
